== QuickStart Guide

This chapter guides you over the basics of DUSTY-12 development. It provides a surface view of the Comm Link system, Core Monitor, and DUSTY-12 assembly language. It has everything you need to start. But you will not find any in-depth information here - for a more comprehensive description and detailed technical specifications, reference other sections of this manual.

=== Your First Steps

The first day you start at any of the Outer Rim League (ORL) companies, you will be connected to CommLink. It is a standard communication system based on the TRUST-8 protocol. It allows you to send and receive messages among other members of the ORL as well as access ORL databanks and bulletin boards.

Locate the CommLink display - it should show your InBox with a Welcome email that you should've received shortly after joining. It is an introductory message common among the outer rim companies.

image::shots/inbox-welcome.png[Welcome Message in InBox]

You can see who it is from, the subject line, and also the exact day and hour the message was received. The star to the left indicates that the message was not read yet.

Click on it and read the content.

image::shots/email-welcome.png[Email view on the Welcome Message]

The message will be shown in a separate tab, and you can close it when no longer needed.

There will be more messages covering the current state of affairs, some technical details, and instructions. Read them all.

=== First Experiment

Eventually, you will receive a Request for an Experiment. It is the moment you are waiting for. The most significant step in your career. Your first programming task!

image::shots/inbox-first-request.png[InBox - First Request]

The experiment will be encoded as S1E1 (Series 1 Experiment 1) and will require you to enable the first telemetry line.

image::shots/email-first-request.png[First Request Email]

It is a very simple task. You already have a DUSTY-12 instruction OBUS (stands for Open data BUS) unlocked - the details should already be in your InBox.

Now is the time for programming.

Locate the Core Monitor display. It shows the code for the current DUSTY-12 capsule and should be empty at the moment. If not, locate and click the "Clear current capsule" button.

image::shots/core-monitor.png[Core Monitor]

You can select any of the memory cells. Click on the one at address 0x000. The Code Selector section to the right will show the possible options, which include the list of hexadecimal numbers and all DUSTY-12 instructions unlocked so far.

Click on any of the available options to set the memory cell. More experienced engineers prefer to use right-click to auto-advance the cursor to the next memory address.

We've been requested to open telemetry data line #0. So let's place the dataline number on the stack first - select 0x00 as the first instruction. Next we need to tell DUSTY-12 to open the data line by providing the OBUS instruction. That instruction will pop the top value on the data stack (0x00) and open the telemetry dataline corresponding to that value.

The whole program will look like this:

```
00
OBUS
```

Or when typed into the Core Monitor:

image::shots/core-monitor-S1E1-solution.png[S1E1 Solution]

You can control Core Monitor with a row of control buttons:

image::shots/debug-monitor-controls.png[Core Monitor Controls]

They are from left to right:

* Clear current capsule
* Stop execution
* Pause execution
* One step forward
* Walk the program (slow execution)
* Run the program (fast execution)

On this stage, we recommend running DUSTY-12 in the Walk mode, or even in the step-by-step mode, carefully examining the effect of each instruction.

Press the "Walk Program" button and observe how the red cursor highlights an instruction to be executed. The program stops when it reaches an empty memory cell.

After the walk, the probe telemetry dataline #0 will start to send telemetry over the engineering channel. You should see the probe wireframe model approximated from that data.

image::shots/telemetry-0.png[Telemetry DataLine #0]


Congratulations!

Your first experiment is complete, and you should receive your reward shortly (there will be an email notification in your InBox).

image::shots/email-S1E1-complete.png["Attitude telemetry experiment is complete" email]

Take your hard-earned money and grab some coffee. It is only the beginning. There is more than one experiment coming along the way!


=== Non-Trivial Challenge

The previous example is one of the simplest programs one can imagine - just placing a single number on the data stack and executing an instruction with that number as a parameter.

To illustrate more complex features of DUSTY-12 and demonstrate the creation of less trivial programs, let's spike the complexity level and create a program that opens data and power lines #0 up to #7. It is a significant leap from the previous example that presents more advanced coding techniques. Don't worry, if the code looks strange and unfamiliar at first - only the most experienced spaceframe engineers can figure it out from the get-go.

The section covers some advanced instructions not available in early experiments. So don't try to repeat this at the beginning of your career. Down the line, You'll encounter similar experiments. This example here is only to show what can be achieved when more advanced DUSTY-12 instructions are unlocked.

Given that we already know about opening datalines with OBUS and there is a similar instruction OPOW to open a powerline, it should be easy to open and power them all:

```
00
OBUS
00
OPOW
01
OBUS
01
OPOW
...
```

Or a little more sophisticated way that includes the usage of the DUP operation that duplicates the top value on the data stack:

```
00
DUP
OBUS
OPOW
01
DUP
OBUS
OPOW
...
```

It may work for 1 or 2 lines, maybe even 3. But once we get to bigger numbers, we'll end up with many snippets of similarly structured code with only slight changes (like line numbers). It will look heavy and non-maintainable. And you will learn fast that when it comes to deep space operations, maintenance is paramount.

.DRY
****
Space is a dry place. There is no atmospheric pressure in the hard vacuum. So any exposed water molecules instantly evaporate and drift through the void. That is why we, outer rim spacecraft engineers, use the DRY principle.

DRY stands for Don't Repeat Yourself and guides us to detect and eliminate any unnecessary duplications.

We duplicate a lot for a reason - computers, propulsion, and communication systems of our spacecraft are often duplicated to provide additional reliability. But when it comes to code, duplication causes many problems. Heavily duplicated code is harder to debug and keep in sync. That is why we always try to keep it DRY.

Don't be shy, keep it DRY!
****

To simplify our approach and follow the DRY principle, all we need is a simple loop. We can use the loop counter to open the corresponding data and power lines.

The following screenshot demonstrates such a loop:

image::shots/simple-loop.png[Simple Loop]

Let's examine the details of implementation.

We place the number 0x08 on the data stack first. This is our loop counter. We want to make the action (open a data and power lines) x8 times in total.

When we program on such a low level, it is more convenient to make the loop from the initial count value down to zero. And loop it with the JNZ instruction that jumps if the value on top is not zero. Once we reach zero, JNZ will let us through, and the loop is over.

After the initial conditions, we enter the loop. The loop counter is reduced. This structure is more preferable for this particular loop, since we want to use values 7 down to 0 (including 0) and only then finish the loop. Alternatives would require additional math and stack manipulations.

On the first cycle, we'll end up with 0x07 at the top of the data stack. That is both the data/power line number and the loop counter value we want to test with JNZ.

But each command will "eat" the top value on the data stack. Therefore, we have to DUP it each time we want to use it (to preserve another copy deeper in the stack for future use).

That is why we do DUP before each open instruction:

```
DUP
OBUS
DUP
OPOW
```

Now, when the data and power lines are open, we will jump to the address 0x01 if the test value is not zero.

```
DUP
01
JNZ
```

Since the first cycle, the value will be 0x07, we should jump back to 0x01 and repeat those actions again - subtracting 0x01, opening the corresponding data and power lines, testing the end condition\...

Once the loop counter reaches 0x00, we exit the loop. There is nothing after, so now the program is over.

Good work!

You've managed to get through the most complex section of the QuickStart Guide.



